WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:06.140
There's a lot to say about VSAM.

2
00:00:06.140 --> 00:00:10.500
I've seen multi-part presentations on the topic of VSAM.

3
00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:15.300
There are courses on VSAM alone that run four to five days.

4
00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:20.140
We've got a couple minutes, so let's get going.

5
00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:21.400
We'll start with the basics.

6
00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:25.260
VSAM stands for Virtual Storage Access Method.

7
00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:27.620
It's a way of storing and accessing data

8
00:00:27.620 --> 00:00:30.900
that's different from what we've been using up until now.

9
00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:33.740
So why do we need another type of data set?

10
00:00:33.740 --> 00:00:36.020
The ones we were looking at before made perfect sense.

11
00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:38.940
I open it up, I put my data in, I close it.

12
00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:40.660
The data is still there next week.

13
00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:42.260
All is good, right?

14
00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:44.140
Well, for you and I, sure.

15
00:00:44.140 --> 00:00:46.380
But what about the applications?

16
00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:49.660
Applications generally aren't reading blogs, writing novels,

17
00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:51.340
scrolling social media.

18
00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:53.620
They've got a specific piece of data

19
00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:56.540
that they've got to get to present to their user.

20
00:00:56.540 --> 00:00:59.380
Or they've got to make an update on a very specific piece

21
00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:01.980
of data in a very specific place.

22
00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:04.780
VSAM gives applications a method of reading

23
00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:07.420
and writing data that makes sense for them.

24
00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:10.380
In fact, if you were to try and open up a VSAM data set

25
00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:13.020
in ISPF, all it would do is tell you,

26
00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:15.140
yep, that's a VSAM data set,

27
00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:18.100
because it's not readable by standard methods.

28
00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:19.780
So how does this work?

29
00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:22.460
Well, like the other data sets we've talked about,

30
00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:24.420
VSAM has records.

31
00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:26.340
In VSAM, those records are kept

32
00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:28.620
in what's called a control interval.

33
00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:31.540
And a control interval is a continuous area

34
00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:33.500
of storage on DASD.

35
00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:35.580
And they can be different sizes.

36
00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:38.980
So when an application goes to read a control interval,

37
00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:41.580
that specific area of storage gets read

38
00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:46.180
into the VSAM I-O buffer so the application can read it.

39
00:01:46.180 --> 00:01:48.940
VSAM has four types of data sets,

40
00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:50.940
based around four different ways

41
00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:53.300
that applications generally like to consume

42
00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:55.180
and record data.

43
00:01:55.180 --> 00:01:58.400
They are keyed sequence data sets.

44
00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:00.640
Entry sequence data sets.

45
00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:02.440
Relative record data sets.

46
00:02:02.440 --> 00:02:04.960
And linear data sets.

47
00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:06.740
Okay, one at a time.

48
00:02:06.740 --> 00:02:10.440
Keyed sequence data sets, or KSDS.

49
00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:15.240
In a KSDS, each record is identified by a unique key.

50
00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:18.640
When you write new data, you create a key for it.

51
00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:21.520
For example, if this were an automobile assembly line,

52
00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:25.980
the index might be a unique vehicle identification number.

53
00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:28.180
And the data portion holds what features

54
00:02:28.180 --> 00:02:30.500
that car is being built with, what color it is,

55
00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:33.720
which dealership it's going to, all that stuff.

56
00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:36.860
Whenever we want that specific piece of information,

57
00:02:36.860 --> 00:02:38.760
we look it up by the key.

58
00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:43.060
This is the most commonly used type of VSAM data set.

59
00:02:43.060 --> 00:02:45.740
And then there's entry sequence data sets.

60
00:02:45.740 --> 00:02:50.220
In an ESDS, each new entry goes in right after the last one

61
00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:53.660
and records are referenced by the relative byte address.

62
00:02:53.660 --> 00:02:56.940
So if we know the records are 64 bytes each,

63
00:02:56.940 --> 00:03:01.020
and we want to reference the first record, the RBA is zero.

64
00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:02.920
And for the second, it's 64.

65
00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:05.820
And for the third one, it's 128, and so on.

66
00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:08.420
Because the records go in one right after the other,

67
00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:09.740
and that's their structure,

68
00:03:09.740 --> 00:03:12.060
you can't delete a record once it's in there.

69
00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:14.720
You can only mark it as inactive.

70
00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:17.700
This structure generally lends itself to high performance

71
00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:21.100
when data is loaded and read sequentially.

72
00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:24.460
Now, an RRDS or relative record data set

73
00:03:24.460 --> 00:03:26.580
is similar to an ESDS,

74
00:03:26.580 --> 00:03:28.380
except that the records are referenced

75
00:03:28.380 --> 00:03:31.840
by the relative record number or RRN.

76
00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:33.980
This number is how many records down

77
00:03:33.980 --> 00:03:35.300
from the first record we are,

78
00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:37.580
similar to rows in a spreadsheet.

79
00:03:37.580 --> 00:03:40.420
And like a spreadsheet, we can have empty records,

80
00:03:40.420 --> 00:03:42.900
we can delete records, we can jump around,

81
00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:44.820
or we can go sequentially.

82
00:03:44.820 --> 00:03:47.060
Lastly, there's linear data sets.

83
00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:48.780
These aren't used as often,

84
00:03:48.780 --> 00:03:51.380
but are still helpful when dealing with byte stream data,

85
00:03:51.380 --> 00:03:54.220
like logs, or where an application will be managing

86
00:03:54.220 --> 00:03:56.660
the data after it grabs it.

87
00:03:56.660 --> 00:03:59.500
Let's test your understanding of these VSAM data set types

88
00:03:59.500 --> 00:04:01.180
with a little pop quiz.

89
00:04:01.180 --> 00:04:03.780
Let's say I need a VSAM data set

90
00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:06.140
to record data in sequence.

91
00:04:06.140 --> 00:04:08.460
And I just happen to know that the program

92
00:04:08.460 --> 00:04:12.100
that processes this data is going to read it in sequence

93
00:04:12.100 --> 00:04:14.020
without skipping around.

94
00:04:14.020 --> 00:04:16.720
What type of data set should I be looking into?

95
00:04:17.720 --> 00:04:22.200
The answer is an ESDS, or entry sequence data set.

96
00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:24.760
Because members go in based on when they're recorded

97
00:04:24.760 --> 00:04:27.700
and we have no need to reference individual records

98
00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:29.660
by name or relative number,

99
00:04:29.660 --> 00:04:33.420
we can make efficient use of an ESDS here.

100
00:04:33.420 --> 00:04:35.960
No matter what data set type you're using,

101
00:04:35.960 --> 00:04:38.440
they usually get created using an IBM utility

102
00:04:38.440 --> 00:04:41.720
called ID cams through JCL.

103
00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:44.200
In your JCL, you'll specify the cluster,

104
00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:47.200
that's the logical representation of that data set,

105
00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:49.600
and all that goes along with it.

106
00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:51.560
So in this example here,

107
00:04:51.560 --> 00:04:54.800
you can see we're invoking the ID cams program

108
00:04:54.800 --> 00:04:59.800
and creating a cluster with the name example.ksds2.

109
00:05:00.440 --> 00:05:01.680
If I were a betting man,

110
00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:04.920
I'd say this is a key sequence data set.

111
00:05:04.920 --> 00:05:06.320
And not just because of the name,

112
00:05:06.320 --> 00:05:10.240
but because down here we're specifying the key length.

113
00:05:10.240 --> 00:05:13.640
Here it's six bytes starting at the fifth byte,

114
00:05:13.640 --> 00:05:17.640
byte four since we start from zero, of the data record.

115
00:05:17.640 --> 00:05:20.320
We need to provide the information for the cluster itself

116
00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:24.040
as well as the data and index of the cluster.

117
00:05:24.040 --> 00:05:26.880
In this example, we're also specifying which catalog

118
00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:29.040
we wanna keep the cluster in.

119
00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:32.720
You can also use JCL itself to create VSAM data sets,

120
00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:35.960
in which case you specify the record organization,

121
00:05:35.960 --> 00:05:37.920
record parameter.

122
00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:40.720
The fundamentals, however, stay the same.

123
00:05:40.720 --> 00:05:42.880
So that's a little bit about VSAM.

124
00:05:42.880 --> 00:05:43.720
I gotta tell you,

125
00:05:43.720 --> 00:05:46.540
this is one of those things that everybody uses.

126
00:05:46.540 --> 00:05:48.960
It's very prevalent in the mainframe community.

127
00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:50.440
It's also one of those things

128
00:05:50.440 --> 00:05:53.240
that's fairly unique to the Z platform.

129
00:05:53.240 --> 00:05:56.440
And there's a lot of performance and code simplification

130
00:05:56.440 --> 00:05:58.040
to be gained by using it.

131
00:05:58.040 --> 00:06:00.160
So when you're done with this course

132
00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:02.040
and you're trying to take the next step,

133
00:06:02.040 --> 00:06:04.080
make sure you read further into it.

134
00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:06.880
You can never have too much VSAM knowledge.
